      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm trying to set up some reorder logic for my shop. we sell small electronics components and i'm worried about running out of lithium battery cells during our peak season. can you give me a basic formula or some rules to help me figure out how much safety stock i should keep on hand to avoid stockouts?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A simple formula is the classic safety stock equation: (Average Daily Usage × Lead Time) + (Safety Factor × Standard Deviation of Demand). For your lithium batteries, you'd want to plug in your peak season usage rates and the time it takes for a new shipment to arrive.\n\nHowever, since you're dealing with small electronics components, you might want to add a \"panic buffer\" for when the supply chain gets weird, like a sudden shortage of raw materials or a logistics delay.\n\nThink of it like a well-stocked goblin hoard; if you run out of the shiny copper coins you need for the alchemy, the whole operation grinds to a halt. You need enough stock to cover the gap between when you need the batteries and when the next shipment arrives, plus a little extra just in case the goblins in the supply chain decide to hide the crates.\n\nHere's a quick rule of thumb: Calculate your average daily sales during peak season, multiply that by your supplier's lead time in days, and then add 20% to 30% as your safety stock. This extra chunk ensures you don't get caught off guard by unexpected spikes in demand or delays.\n\nIf you're worried about",
